---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import Rounding from '@/components/tools/calc2/Rounding.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="rounding-calculator">
  <Rounding client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the rounding calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      The rounding calculator finds the two neighbours of your number at the precision you choose, then
      picks one by the rounding rule. It works on the digits as typed, so 1.005 to two decimal places gives
      1.01. Calculators that use binary floating point store 1.005 as 1.00499999… and give 1.00.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>2.5</th><th>3.5</th><th>−2.5</th><th>2.4</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Half up (away from zero)</td><td>3</td><td>4</td><td>−3</td><td>2</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Half to even</td><td>2</td><td>4</td><td>−2</td><td>2</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Up (ceiling)</td><td>3</td><td>4</td><td>−2</td><td>3</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Down (floor)</td><td>2</td><td>3</td><td>−3</td><td>2</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Toward zero</td><td>2</td><td>3</td><td>−2</td><td>2</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      To round 0.125 to two decimal places, the neighbours are 0.12 and 0.13 and the value is exactly
      halfway. Half up gives 0.13; half to even gives 0.12, because 2 is even. To round 1,234.5 to the
      nearest 100 (−2 places), the neighbours are 1,200 and 1,300 and the value is below halfway: 1,200.
    </p>

    <h2>Why half to even</h2>
    <p>
      Half up always moves the halfway cases up, so a long column of rounded values adds up too high. Half
      to even moves half of them up and half down, so the error cancels. Banks and the IEEE 754 standard
      use it.
    </p>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <p>
      Round once, at the end. Rounding an already rounded value can give a different answer: 1.445 to two
      places is 1.45, then to one place 1.5, but 1.445 to one place directly is 1.4.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
